<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="side-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find a Missing Side of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>A right triangle side calculator finds the unknown length in a right triangle when you know the other two sides. Common uses include measuring room diagonals, checking support beams, or verifying homework answers. The calculator needs exactly two side lengths to work. It applies the Pythagorean theorem relating the three sides of every right triangle. Finding the hypotenuse uses addition under a square root, while finding a missing leg uses subtraction. The method works only for right triangles, as the relationship depends on the 90-degree angle. For example, a right triangle with perpendicular sides of 6 cm and 8 cm has a hypotenuse of 10 cm. This same principle measures real distances, from ladder lengths to screen diagonals.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding Sides -->
<section id="side-anatomy" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Three Sides of a Right Triangle</h2>
  
  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle contains one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees, the same as the corner of a square or rectangle. The other two angles must be acute, meaning each measures less than 90 degrees. Since triangle angles sum to 180 degrees, the two non-right angles total 90 degrees. This differs from acute triangles (all angles under 90°) and obtuse triangles (one angle over 90°). The right angle creates the perpendicular relationship behind the Pythagorean theorem.</p>
  
  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse sits opposite the right angle, stretching across from the 90-degree corner. It is always the longest side because it spans the greatest distance between any two vertices. In rotated diagrams, the side not touching the right-angle marker is the hypotenuse. This calculator labels it c, following the standard convention where c denotes the hypotenuse in the formula <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />.</p>
  
  <h3>What Are the Two Legs?</h3>
  <p>The legs are the two sides that form the right angle, meeting at 90 degrees. They are labeled a and b in this calculator. Swapping labels does not change the hypotenuse, since a² + b² equals b² + a². The calculator keeps them separate so the missing-leg modes know which leg to solve for. When you know both legs, either order works for finding the hypotenuse.</p>
  
  <h3>How Can You Identify the Correct Side?</h3>
  <p>Locate the right-angle marker (the small square). The opposite side is the hypotenuse; the two touching sides are the legs. For rotated diagrams, imagine turning the triangle until the right angle sits at the bottom left with one leg horizontal and the other vertical. Without a right-angle marker, confirm the triangle is right-angled before using this calculator.</p>
  
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xl" set:html={inlineSideSvg('side-anatomy.svg')} />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Parts of a right triangle: legs a and b form the right angle at C, hypotenuse c sits opposite, with acute angles A and B.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Pythagorean Theorem -->
<section id="side-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Pythagorean Theorem Calculates Triangle Sides</h2>
  <p>The Pythagorean theorem states that <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />, where a and b are the legs and c is the hypotenuse. Each variable is a side length. Squaring multiplies a number by itself (6² = 36); the square root reverses this (√100 = 10). You take the root because the formula relates squared lengths, not lengths themselves. Adding leg lengths (6 + 8 = 14) is wrong; add their squares (36 + 64 = 100), then take the root. The theorem needs the 90-degree angle; non-right triangles require the Law of Cosines. All three calculator modes rearrange this equation: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{c^2 - a^2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{c^2 - b^2}]]')} />.</p>
  
  <h3>Understanding Why the Formula Works</h3>
  <p>Draw a square on each side: areas a², b², and c². The theorem says the two smaller areas sum to the largest. For 3-4-5: squares of 9 and 16 square units sum to 25, the hypotenuse square area. Squaring matters because you compare areas, not lengths.</p>
  
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xl" set:html={inlineSideSvg('side-pythagoras-squares.svg')} />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Visual proof: the 9 and 16 square-unit squares on the legs combine to equal the 25 square-unit square on the hypotenuse.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Finding Hypotenuse -->
<section id="side-hypotenuse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find the Hypotenuse Using Two Legs</h2>
  <p>Solving <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} /> for c gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}]]')} />. The process: identify both legs, square each, add the squares, take the root, write the unit, and confirm the result exceeds both legs.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 1 — Finding the Hypotenuse of a 6-8 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given legs a = 6 cm and b = 8 cm: square each (36 and 64), add (100), take the root (10). The hypotenuse is 10 cm, exceeding both legs as expected.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5" set:html={rich('[[[c = \\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100} = 10\\text{ cm}]]]')} />
  
  <h3>Example 2 — Finding a Hypotenuse With Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given a = 2.5 m, b = 6 m: 2.5² + 6² = 6.25 + 36 = 42.25, √42.25 = 6.5 m. Decimals need careful arithmetic; keep full precision until the final rounding.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5" set:html={rich('[[[c = \\sqrt{2.5^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{6.25 + 36} = \\sqrt{42.25} = 6.5\\text{ m}]]]')} />
  
  <h3>When Should You Use This Calculator Mode?</h3>
  <p>Use hypotenuse mode with two known legs: room diagonals, support braces, or geometry problems giving perpendicular sides. With hypotenuse plus one leg, switch to missing-leg mode.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Finding Missing Leg -->
<section id="side-leg" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find a Missing Leg of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>When you know the hypotenuse and one leg, rearrange the Pythagorean theorem to isolate the unknown leg. Starting from <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />, subtract the known leg squared from both sides. This gives two formulas, one for each leg.</p>
  
  <h3>Find Leg b When Hypotenuse c and Leg a Are Known</h3>
  <p>The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{c^2 - a^2}]]')} />. Derive it: begin with <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />, subtract a² from both sides to get <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c^2 - a^2]]')} />, then take the square root. The subtraction reflects that the hypotenuse squared contains both leg squares, so removing one leaves the other.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 3 — Find Leg b From a 13 cm Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given c = 13 cm and a = 5 cm, find leg b. Square both: 13² = 169 and 5² = 25. Subtract: 169 − 25 = 144. Take the root: √144 = 12. Leg b measures 12 cm. Verify: 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13². The triple 5-12-13 confirms the result.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5" set:html={rich('[[[b = \\sqrt{13^2 - 5^2} = \\sqrt{169 - 25} = \\sqrt{144} = 12\\text{ cm}]]]')} />
  
  <h3>Find Leg a When Hypotenuse c and Leg b Are Known</h3>
  <p>The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{c^2 - b^2}]]')} />. The legs are interchangeable in the Pythagorean relationship; labeling one as a and the other as b is a convention. The calculator offers both modes so you can match the field labels to your specific problem without mentally swapping variables.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 4 — Finding the Unknown Leg</h3>
  <p>Given c = 17 inches and b = 15 inches, find leg a. Square: 17² = 289 and 15² = 225. Subtract: 289 − 225 = 64. Take the root: √64 = 8. Leg a is 8 inches. This is the 8-15-17 triple. The two missing-leg modes differ only in which leg is known; the mathematics is identical.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5" set:html={rich('[[[a = \\sqrt{17^2 - 15^2} = \\sqrt{289 - 225} = \\sqrt{64} = 8\\text{ inches}]]]')} />
  
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Known</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Find</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Formula</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Calculator Mode</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
