      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xl" set:html={inlineSideSvg('side-missing-leg.svg')} />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Two missing-leg calculations: finding b from the 5-12-13 triangle and finding a from the 8-15-17 triangle.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Using the Calculator -->
<section id="side-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Side Calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the two measurements you know.</strong> They must both be side lengths, not angles.</li>
    <li><strong>Determine whether the missing value is the hypotenuse or a leg.</strong> If you know both legs, you need the hypotenuse. If you know the hypotenuse and one leg, you need the other leg.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose the correct mode.</strong> Select "Pythagorean Theorem" for two known legs, "Missing Leg From c and a" when you know the hypotenuse and leg a, or "Missing Leg From c and b" when you know the hypotenuse and leg b.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter both measurements into their matching fields.</strong> Double-check that each value goes into the correct field; swapping the hypotenuse with a leg produces an error.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose the measurement unit.</strong> Use the same unit for both inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculate.</strong> The result appears with the solution steps showing the substitution and arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the result and steps.</strong> Confirm the formula matches your situation and the arithmetic looks correct.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that the answer makes sense.</strong> The hypotenuse must exceed both legs. A missing leg must be shorter than the hypotenuse. If these fail, recheck your inputs.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator does not save results or generate downloadable reports. Use the reset option to clear fields for a new calculation.</p>
</section>

