<!-- Section 8: Real-Life Applications -->
<section id="side-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Examples of Right Triangle Side Calculations</h2>
  
  <h3>Example 5 — Ladder Against a Wall</h3>
  <p>A ladder reaches 2.4 m vertically up a wall, with its base 1.8 m from the wall. The wall and ground form a right angle, creating a right triangle where the ladder is the hypotenuse. Known: a = 1.8 m (base), b = 2.4 m (height). Find: c (ladder length). Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}]]')} />. Substitute: c = √(1.8² + 2.4²) = √(3.24 + 5.76) = √9 = 3. The ladder measures 3 m. This tells you the ladder length needed to reach that height at that distance. Note: this geometry does not assess ladder safety; follow manufacturer guidelines and safety regulations for actual use.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 6 — Diagonal of a Rectangular Room</h3>
  <p>A room measures 9 ft long and 12 ft wide. The diagonal between opposite corners forms the hypotenuse of a right triangle with the walls as legs. Known: a = 9 ft, b = 12 ft. Find: c. Formula: c = √(9² + 12²) = √(81 + 144) = √225 = 15. The diagonal is 15 ft. This helps when checking if a room is square (both diagonals should match) or determining the longest straight-line distance for furniture placement.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 7 — Roof Rafter Length</h3>
  <p>A simplified roof cross-section has a horizontal run of 12 ft and vertical rise of 5 ft. The rafter forms the hypotenuse. Known: run = 12 ft, rise = 5 ft. Find: rafter length. Formula: c = √(12² + 5²) = √(144 + 25) = √169 = 13. The rafter is 13 ft long. This basic calculation excludes overhangs, ridge thickness, and connection allowances that real construction requires. Consult building plans for complete specifications.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 8 — TV or Monitor Diagonal</h3>
  <p>A rectangular display measures 16 inches wide and 9 inches tall. The diagonal is the hypotenuse. Known: width = 16 in, height = 9 in. Find: diagonal. Formula: d = √(16² + 9²) = √(256 + 81) = √337 ≈ 18.36. The diagonal measures approximately 18.36 inches. This uses the stated dimensions; commercial screen sizes may differ based on manufacturer measurement methods.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 9 — Measuring a Ramp</h3>
  <p>A ramp has a horizontal run of 6 m and a rise of 0.75 m. The sloping surface is the hypotenuse. Known: run = 6 m, rise = 0.75 m. Find: slope length. Formula: s = √(6² + 0.75²) = √(36 + 0.5625) = √36.5625 ≈ 6.05. The slope measures about 6.05 m. The horizontal run (6 m) differs from the slope length (6.05 m); the slope is always slightly longer. This does not verify accessibility compliance; check local building codes for ramp requirements.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 10 — Rectangular Garden Path</h3>
  <p>A rectangular garden is 30 ft wide and 40 ft long. A diagonal path cuts across. Known: a = 30 ft, b = 40 ft. Find: path length. Formula: c = √(30² + 40²) = √(900 + 1600) = √2500 = 50. The path is 50 ft. Walking the diagonal (50 ft) is shorter than walking along two sides (70 ft), which is why diagonal paths save time.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 11 — Support Brace With a Known Diagonal</h3>
  <p>A support brace measures 10 ft long (hypotenuse) and spans 8 ft horizontally. Find the vertical distance. Known: c = 10 ft, a = 8 ft. Find: b. Formula: b = √(c² − a²) = √(100 − 64) = √36 = 6. The vertical distance is 6 ft. Subtraction is necessary because the hypotenuse squared contains both leg squares; removing the known horizontal component leaves the vertical.</p>
  
  <h3>Example 12 — Engineering Drawing With Decimal Values</h3>
  <p>A bracket design specifies a hypotenuse of 250 mm and one leg of 70 mm. Find the other leg. Known: c = 250 mm, b = 70 mm. Find: a. Formula: a = √(c² − b²) = √(62500 − 4900) = √57600 = 240. The leg measures 240 mm. With millimeter inputs, the result is exact here, but in practice round to the precision of your least precise measurement. Do not report more decimal places than your tools can measure.</p>
  
