<!-- Section 9: Pythagorean Triples -->
<section id="side-triples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Pythagorean Triples and Why They Matter</h2>
  <p>A Pythagorean triple consists of three whole numbers that satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. These triples provide quick mental checks; if your inputs match a triple, you know the answer without calculating. Scaled triples also work: multiplying 3-4-5 by 2 gives 6-8-10, which still satisfies the equation (36 + 64 = 100). Not every right triangle has whole-number sides; most produce decimals or irrational numbers.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[400px] border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Triple</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Verification</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">3-4-5</td><td class="py-2">9 + 16 = 25 ✓</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">5-12-13</td><td class="py-2">25 + 144 = 169 ✓</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">8-15-17</td><td class="py-2">64 + 225 = 289 ✓</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">7-24-25</td><td class="py-2">49 + 576 = 625 ✓</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">9-40-41</td><td class="py-2">81 + 1600 = 1681 ✓</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15 last:border-0"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">6-8-10</td><td class="py-2">36 + 64 = 100 ✓ (scaled 3-4-5)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Verify 5-12-13: 5² = 25, 12² = 144, 25 + 144 = 169, √169 = 13. Verify 8-15-17: 8² = 64, 15² = 225, 64 + 225 = 289, √289 = 17. When your calculator returns a whole number, check if it forms a triple with your inputs as a sanity check.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Units and Precision -->
<section id="side-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Measurement Units, Decimal Answers, and Rounding</h2>
  <p>Use consistent units: millimeters, centimeters, meters, inches, feet, yards, or kilometers. Mixed units produce wrong answers; 3 feet and 6 inches must become 3.5 feet or 42 inches before calculating. Convert first, then compute. Select the matching unit in the calculator for both inputs. Round only after the final calculation, not during intermediate steps, to avoid compounding errors. An exact answer like 10 cm differs from a rounded decimal like 6.50 m; the latter implies measurement precision. Your result cannot be more precise than your least precise input. If you measure to the nearest centimeter, do not report millimeters.</p>
  <p>Example with feet and inches: a board measures 2 ft 6 in by 3 ft. Convert to inches: 30 in and 36 in. Calculate: c = √(30² + 36²) = √(900 + 1296) = √2196 ≈ 46.86 in. Convert back: 46.86 in = 3 ft 10.86 in, or about 3 ft 10.9 in.</p>
  <p>Note: squaring a length produces square units (cm²), but the final square root returns linear units (cm). The calculator handles this automatically.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Invalid Inputs -->
<section id="side-invalid" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Some Triangle Measurements Cannot Produce a Valid Answer</h2>
  <p>A valid right triangle needs three positive side lengths where the hypotenuse exceeds each leg. For missing-leg calculations, the value under the square root (c² − leg²) must be positive.</p>
  <p><strong>Invalid:</strong> c = 8, a = 10. The hypotenuse (8) is shorter than the leg (10). This violates the hypotenuse-is-longest rule. You likely swapped the labels; the 10 should be the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><strong>Invalid:</strong> c = 5, a = 5. Then b = √(25 − 25) = √0 = 0. A zero-length side collapses the triangle into a line segment, not a valid triangle.</p>
  <p><strong>Invalid:</strong> a = −3, b = 4. Negative lengths have no geometric meaning. Side lengths must be positive.</p>
