<!-- Section 12: Verification -->
<section id="side-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Calculated Side Is Correct</h2>
  <p><strong>Method 1: Substitute back.</strong> Plug all three sides into <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. For the 6-8-10 triangle: 36 + 64 = 100. If both sides match, the answer is correct.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 2: Check the hypotenuse is longest.</strong> If you solved for c, confirm it exceeds both a and b. If you solved for a leg, confirm it is shorter than c.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 3: Compare with triples.</strong> If your inputs are whole numbers, see if they form a known triple. Inputs 9 and 12 giving 15 matches the scaled 3-4-5 triple.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 4: Estimate reasonableness.</strong> The hypotenuse should be longer than either leg but shorter than their sum. For legs 6 and 8, the hypotenuse 10 satisfies 8 &lt; 10 &lt; 14.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 5: Check units and rounding.</strong> Confirm the unit matches your inputs and the decimal places reflect your measurement precision.</p>
  <p><strong>Verification Example 1:</strong> You calculate c = 13 from a = 5, b = 12. Check: 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13². The hypotenuse 13 exceeds both legs. This matches the 5-12-13 triple. Valid.</p>
  <p><strong>Verification Example 2:</strong> You calculate b = 7.5 from c = 12.5, a = 10. Check: 10² + 7.5² = 100 + 56.25 = 156.25 = 12.5². The leg 7.5 is shorter than hypotenuse 12.5. Estimate: 10 &lt; 12.5 &lt; 20. Valid.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Common Mistakes -->
<section id="side-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Sides</h2>
  <p><strong>1. Adding the legs instead of their squares.</strong> Computing 6 + 8 = 14 instead of √(36 + 64) = 10. The theorem adds squared areas, not lengths. Always square first.</p>
  <p><strong>2. Forgetting the square root.</strong> Answering 100 instead of 10 for the 6-8 triangle. The formula gives c²; you need c. Take the root as the final step.</p>
  <p><strong>3. Subtracting when finding the hypotenuse.</strong> Using √(64 − 36) = √28 instead of √(64 + 36) = 10. Addition applies when finding the hypotenuse from two legs.</p>
  <p><strong>4. Adding when finding a missing leg.</strong> Using √(169 + 25) instead of √(169 − 25) = 12. Subtraction applies when the hypotenuse is known.</p>
  <p><strong>5. Mistaking a leg for the hypotenuse.</strong> Labeling the longest side as a or b. The hypotenuse is always opposite the right angle and always labeled c.</p>
  <p><strong>6. Applying the theorem to a non-right triangle.</strong> Using a² + b² = c² without a 90-degree angle. Verify the right angle first.</p>
  <p><strong>7. Rounding intermediate calculations.</strong> Rounding 6.25 to 6.3 mid-calculation, then getting a slightly wrong final answer. Keep full precision until the end.</p>
  <p><strong>8. Mixing measurement units.</strong> Entering 3 feet and 6 inches as 3 and 6. Convert to a single unit before calculating.</p>
  <p><strong>9. Entering values into wrong fields.</strong> Putting the hypotenuse in a leg field. Match each measurement to its correct label.</p>
  <p><strong>10. Misinterpreting a negative under the root.</strong> Seeing √(25 − 36) and forcing a calculation. A negative radicand means the inputs cannot form a right triangle; check your labels.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Beyond Pythagoras -->
<section id="side-beyond" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>When Do You Need a Different Right Triangle Formula?</h2>
  <p>The Pythagorean theorem needs two known sides. Different information requires different tools.</p>
  <p><strong>One side and one acute angle:</strong> Use sine, cosine, or tangent. Sine relates opposite over hypotenuse, cosine relates adjacent over hypotenuse, and tangent relates opposite over adjacent. For example, with hypotenuse c and angle A, leg a = c × sin(A).</p>
  <p><strong>Two angles, no sides:</strong> Impossible. Angles determine shape but not size; a 3-4-5 triangle and 6-8-10 triangle share angles but differ in scale. You need at least one side length.</p>
  <p><strong>Non-right triangle:</strong> Use the Law of Cosines (c² = a² + b² − 2ab×cos(C)) or Law of Sines. The Pythagorean theorem is a special case of the Law of Cosines when C = 90°.</p>
  <p><strong>Special right triangles:</strong> The 45-45-90 triangle has legs in ratio 1:1:√2, and the 30-60-90 has sides in ratio 1:√3:2. These allow quick mental calculations.</p>
  <p>If you know one side and one acute angle, use the <a href="/right-triangle-side-from-angle-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Right Triangle Side Calculator Using Angle</a> on this site.</p>
</section>

